<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02141</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e9d7613a-4686-419d-8d71-55a3dab73aaf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>What values of the variables make each equation true?</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise id="_8ad43010-4e4f-445c-9428-d077659343f1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the value of a.</para><para>\(6+2a=0\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(– 3\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is -3 </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5a986493-88af-432e-8330-00139cfbe17e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the value of b.</para><para>\(7b=0\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(0\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_81cbe54c-941e-4369-a118-64ef330c2b6c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the value of c.</para><para>\(7(c−5)=0\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(3\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is 3.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3c031f12-8a5b-4bc4-b1f4-bffffb2e1cd5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Determine which of the statements are true if \(g \cdot h=0\). HINT: There may be more than one statement that is true. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is (g=0\), \(h=0\), and \(g=h=0\). Either \(g=0\) or \(h=0\). But also, notice, if \(g=0\) and \(h=0\) at the same time, you can also say that \(g = h = 0\).</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(g=0\), \(h=0\), and \(g=h=0\)
        Either \(g=0\) or \(h=0\). But also, notice, if \(g=0\) and \(h=0\) at the same time, you can also say that \(g = h = 0\).</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What did you notice about one of the factors in each of the previous problems?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter what you noticed.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> At least one of the factors in each equation was 0 or equivalent to 0.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>We will use the <term class="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term> to help solve <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equations</term> throughout this lesson.</para>

<note>
  <para> ZERO PRODUCT PROPERTY</para>
  <para>If the product of two expressions is 0, then one or both of the expressions equal 0.</para>
  <para>If \(ab=0\), then \(a=0\) or \(b=0\), or \(a=b=0\).</para>
</note></section>
</content>
